# Build your first product

This path starts with an empty Flutter application and ends with one working
vertical slice: Flutter → Query → typed RPC → Server → typed SQL → SQLite.
The same project also contains a D1 migration and a locked local Cloudflare
toolchain. No global Wrangler install is required.

## Create the application

Odroe is currently consumed from source. Keep the checkout and application
beside each other while the next public package release is prepared:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/odroe/odroe.git
flutter create --empty --platforms=android,ios,web my_app
cd my_app
```

Add the checkout as a direct dependency:

```yaml
dependencies:
  odroe:
    path: ../odroe
```

Resolve the dependency, then initialize the complete product in the current
application:

```sh
flutter pub get
dart run odroe init --full-stack
```

`init --full-stack` verifies that this is an empty Flutter application and that
it resolves the same Odroe checkout that is running the command. It preflights
every destination before writing, rejects custom or linked targets, and rolls
the whole operation back on failure. There is deliberately no `--force`.

## Know what was created

```text
my_app/
├── lib/
│   ├── main.dart              # Flutter composition root
│   ├── posts_database.dart    # shared schema and typed query builder
│   ├── rpc_origin.dart        # browser/native origin policy
│   ├── server.dart            # conditional server export
│   ├── server_native.dart     # process-owned SQLite
│   ├── server_cloudflare.dart # invocation-scoped D1
│   ├── routes/
│   │   ├── route.dart         # shared route and semantic document
│   │   ├── page.dart          # Flutter Query + typed RPC
│   │   └── server.dart        # typed RPC + typed SQL
│   ├── routes.dart            # generated client references
│   └── routes.server.dart     # generated server manifest
├── migrations/0001_posts.sql
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
└── wrangler.jsonc
```

The root page asks for post `42`. Native execution seeds `SQLite post 42`;
local D1 seeds `D1 post 42`. The route, function input, function result, SQL
columns, and complete `Post` projection remain typed throughout the path. The
native seed uses `insertOnConflictDoNothing` with `posts.id` as its explicit
conflict target; it does not hide a raw application write inside the starter.

Native SQLite persists at `.odroe/app.sqlite3` by default, and the initializer
adds `.odroe/` to `.gitignore`. Set `ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` to override it; use an
absolute path on writable persistent storage in production. The starter's
idempotent bootstrap preserves existing rows across restarts. The default
relative path is resolved from the server process's working directory.

Run the initializer again at any time. An unchanged starter produces no file
changes. The smaller `dart run odroe init` remains available when a product
needs only Document and Router; an untouched small starter can later upgrade
atomically with `--full-stack`.

## Run on Flutter and the native server

Start both the Odroe development server and Flutter Web:

```sh
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
```

Open the reported Flutter URL. The page renders `SQLite post 42` after Query
calls the generated `posts.read-title` reference. Stop the command with
Ctrl-C; Odroe drains the listener before closing the process-owned database.
Starting the command again reopens the same `.odroe/app.sqlite3` file.

To inspect only the semantic server response, run:

```sh
dart run odroe dev --server-only
```

Visit `http://127.0.0.1:3000/`. Development reads `public/` directly and never
reuses stale HTML from `build/web`.

Browser RPC is same-origin. A native device cannot use the browser's relative
origin, so pass the server origin explicitly:

```sh
flutter devices
dart run odroe dev -- -d <device-id> \
  --dart-define=ODROE_API_ORIGIN=https://api.example.com
```

The generated `rpc_origin.dart` rejects a missing or malformed native origin at
startup rather than failing on the first request.

## Run the edge path locally

Node 22+ and npm 10.9+ are used only by the project-owned Cloudflare toolchain.
They do not enter the Dart dependency graph, Flutter artifact, or Worker
bundle. Run these commands from `my_app`:

```sh
npm ci
dart run odroe build --server-target cloudflare web
npm run cloudflare:migrate:local
npm run cloudflare:dev
```

The migration and Wrangler development scripts include `--local`. The final
command starts local Workerd; `/` serves the semantic handoff and the same
typed RPC reads `D1 post 42` through the invocation's `DB` binding. Removing
`--local` or deploying changes remote state and is a separate operation.

## Build only what you need

```sh
# Flutter Web plus prerendered HTML; no deployable server
dart run odroe build --no-server web

# Flutter WebAssembly plus the same Document handoff
dart run odroe build --no-server -- web --wasm

# Native server only
dart run odroe build --server-only

# Cloudflare Worker only
dart run odroe build --server-only --server-target cloudflare
```

`dev` and `build` regenerate route targets automatically. Use
`dart run odroe generate` for a fast explicit compiler check.

Native prerender is isolated from runtime state. The CLI overrides
`ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` for the prerender child with a temporary database and removes
it after the child stops. The generated starter therefore never reads or writes
the local `.odroe/app.sqlite3` file during a build.

